keep-ram-clean for Debian
=========================

Configuration
-------------
The system-wide daemon reads /etc/keep-ram-clean/config.toml.
Edit trigger thresholds or schedules using:

  sudo keep-ram-clean config show
  sudo keep-ram-clean config set ram-percent 75

Systemd Service
---------------
The system service is installed to /usr/lib/systemd/system/keep-ram-clean.service
and is disabled by default until enabled by the administrator:

  sudo systemctl enable --now keep-ram-clean.service

A non-root user service is also provided at
/usr/lib/systemd/user/keep-ram-clean.service:

  systemctl --user enable --now keep-ram-clean.service

Build scope and provenance
--------------------------
For an upstream convenience build, scripts/build-bookworm.sh takes the exact
committed HEAD (not uncommitted edits), vendors the registry crates selected
by Cargo.lock, and builds native linux/amd64 in a digest-pinned official Rust
1.90.0 Bookworm image. It requires git, tar, Cargo with access to the locked
crates (or a prepopulated offline cache), Docker and the image. The build
container has no network and does not mount the user's home or start services.
Use CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true when the locked crates are cached. Stock Bookworm
rustc/cargo are not the toolchain for this build.

scripts/build-deb.sh is instead a native host convenience builder. It needs
dpkg-dev, binutils, a linker, the installed development/symbols packages and
a Rust toolchain compatible with the locked crates. Ubuntu CI's native
package is an Ubuntu build, not Debian 12 verification. Build in the oldest
supported suite, not on a newer host with a manually lowered Depends.
The Debian source rules retain dh_shlibdeps and shlibs:Depends. They are a
separate path: an archive-ready offline sbuild build with Debian-provided
Rust dependencies has not been established by the convenience build.

The .provenance sidecar records revision, suite, architecture, image/host,
toolchain, Cargo.lock hash and package metadata. The per-package .sha256
covers both artifacts; neither it nor this procedure signs them. Starting
from a trusted copy, use sha256sum -c on that manifest. SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
defaults to the Debian changelog timestamp. To verify an independent build,
use the same commit, image and architecture, and compare both checksums.
scripts/verify-deb.sh recomputes dependencies on the extracted ELF in the
same build suite and optionally enforces a GLIBC symbol ceiling. These are
packaging/ABI checks, not a runtime or service certification.
